# devonf ${TAG}

> **WARNING: UNTESTED ON HARDWARE.**
> These images have not been verified on a real moto g73 5G. They can fail
> to boot, brick the device or lose your data. Only use them if you have the
> complete stock firmware for your exact build and know how to restore it.

## What is included

${COMPONENTS}

Kernel release: `${KERNEL_RELEASE}`

Verify your download before using anything:

```
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
```

## TWRP recovery

Never flash the raw vendor_boot image from this release
(`devonf-twrp-vendor_boot-raw-DO-NOT-FLASH.img`). It lacks the stock vendor
ramdisk (first-stage fstab and kernel modules, including the touchscreen
driver), so a normal boot breaks. Repack the recovery ramdisk into YOUR stock
vendor_boot instead:

```
scripts/extract-stock-images.sh <your-stock-firmware.zip> stock/devonf
scripts/repack-vendor-boot.sh -s stock/devonf/images/vendor_boot.img -r devonf-twrp-recovery-ramdisk.cpio.lz4 -o twrp-vendor_boot.img
scripts/flash.sh recovery twrp-vendor_boot.img
```

To undo, flash your stock vendor_boot.img back to the active slot (see
docs/recovery.md).

## Kernel

```
scripts/repack-boot.sh -s stock/devonf/images/boot.img -k devonf-kernel-Image.gz -o boot-custom.img
fastboot boot boot-custom.img
```

Try the temporary `fastboot boot` first. Only flash it permanently
(`scripts/flash.sh kernel boot-custom.img`) once it works. See docs/kernel.md.

## postmarketOS

This wipes Android user data.

```
unxz devonf-pmos-<ui>-rootfs.img.xz
fastboot getvar current-slot
fastboot flash userdata devonf-pmos-<ui>-rootfs.img
fastboot flash boot_<slot> devonf-pmos-boot.img
fastboot flash vendor_boot_<slot> devonf-pmos-vendor_boot.img
```

Replace `<ui>` with the user interface in the file name and `<slot>` with the
current slot (a or b). The vendor_boot step only applies if that file is part
of this release. `scripts/flash.sh linux` does the boot and userdata steps. See
docs/linux.md.

The default login is `user` / `147147`. Change the password immediately.

## Restore stock

```
scripts/flash.sh restore <firmware-dir>
```

See docs/restore-stock.md.

